    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-and-other-legislation-amendment-omnibus-act-2023-sch-6-sec-19",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "19 Decision by the Attorney-General to refuse consent to transfer",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-and-other-legislation-amendment-omnibus-act-2023#sch-6-sec-19"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "crimes-and-other-legislation-amendment-omnibus-act-2023.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "crimes-and-other-legislation-amendment-omnibus-act-2023.pdf, p.21",
      "values:originalText": "from Australia\n(1) If the transfer country consents to the transfer of the prisoner on\nterms that it proposes, the Attorney-General may decide to refuse\nconsent to the transfer on those terms.\n(2) Before deciding under subsection (1) to refuse consent, the\nAttorney-General must notify the prisoner (or the prisoner’s\nrepresentative) of the proposed terms on which the transfer country\nhas consented to the transfer, including the proposed method by\nwhich the sentence of imprisonment will be enforced by the\ntransfer country.\n(3) If the Attorney-General decides under subsection (1) to refuse\nconsent to the transfer on terms proposed by the transfer country,\nthe Attorney-General:\n(a) must notify the transfer country of the decision; and\n(b) may advise the transfer country in writing that consent may\nbe given if the transfer country proposes a variation of those\nterms, including any variation suggested by the\nAttorney-General.\nNote:\nThis section applies separately for each occasion where the transfer\ncountry proposes varied terms. This means the Attorney-General can\nconsider whether to refuse consent under subsection (1) in relation to\neach variation of terms proposed by the transfer country.\n\nSchedule 6  Refusal of consent to international transfer of prisoners\n\nCrimes and Other Legislation Amendment (Omnibus) Act 2023\nNo. 63, 2023\n(4) If the Attorney-General decides under subsection (1) to refuse\nconsent to the transfer on terms proposed by the transfer country,\nsections 20 to 23 do not apply for the transfer on those particular\nterms.\nNote:\nSections 20 to 23 continue to apply for the transfer on any proposed\nvariation of those terms, unless the Attorney-General has refused\nconsent to the varied terms under subsection (1) of this section.",
      "skos:definition": "from Australia\n(1) If the transfer country consents to the transfer of the prisoner on\nterms that it proposes, the Attorney-General may decide to refuse\nconsent to the transfer on those terms.\n(2) Before deciding under subsection (1) to refuse consent, the\nAttorney-General must notify the prisoner (or the prisoner’s\nrepresentative) of the proposed terms on which the transfer country\nhas consented to the transfer, including the proposed method by\nwhich the sentence of imprisonment will be enforced by the\ntransfer country.\n(3) If the Attorney-General decides under subsection (1) to refuse\nconsent to the transfer on terms proposed by the transfer country,\nthe Attorney-General:\n(a) must notify the transfer country of the decision; and\n(b) may advise the transfer country in writing that consent may\nbe given if the transfer country proposes a variation of those\nterms, including any variation suggested by the\nAttorney-General.\nNote:\nThis section applies separately for each occasion where the transfer\ncountry proposes varied terms. This means the Attorney-General can\nconsider whether to refuse consent under subsection (1) in relation to\neach variation of terms proposed by the transfer country.\n\nSchedule 6  Refusal of consent to international transfer of prisoners\n\nCrimes and Other Legislation Amendment (Omnibus) Act 2023\nNo. 63, 2023\n(4) If the Attorney-General decides under subsection (1) to refuse\nconsent to the transfer on terms proposed by the transfer country,\nsections 20 to 23 do not apply for the transfer on those particular\nterms.\nNote:\nSections 20 to 23 continue to apply for the transfer on any proposed\nvariation of those terms, unless the Attorney-General has refused\nconsent to the varied terms under subsection (1) of this section.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-and-other-legislation-amendment-omnibus-act-2023-sch-6-sec-19-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-and-other-legislation-amendment-omnibus-act-2023-sch-6-sec-19-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-and-other-legislation-amendment-omnibus-act-2023-sch-6-sec-19-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-and-other-legislation-amendment-omnibus-act-2023-sch-6-sec-19-ss-4"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "627711fdd448d13c806ad90f2f2b56f556134bd6643f056f7c3ff9629d9f04e6"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-and-other-legislation-amendment-omnibus-act-2023#sch-6-sec-19",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "19 Decision by the Attorney-General to refuse consent to transfer",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 21,
      "values:originalText": "from Australia\n(1) If the transfer country consents to the transfer of the prisoner on\nterms that it proposes, the Attorney-General may decide to refuse\nconsent to the transfer on those terms.\n(2) Before deciding under subsection (1) to refuse consent, the\nAttorney-General must notify the prisoner (or the prisoner’s\nrepresentative) of the proposed terms on which the transfer country\nhas consented to the transfer, including the proposed method by\nwhich the sentence of imprisonment will be enforced by the\ntransfer country.\n(3) If the Attorney-General decides under subsection (1) to refuse\nconsent to the transfer on terms proposed by the transfer country,\nthe Attorney-General:\n(a) must notify the transfer country of the decision; and\n(b) may advise the transfer country in writing that consent may\nbe given if the transfer country proposes a variation of those\nterms, including any variation suggested by the\nAttorney-General.\nNote:\nThis section applies separately for each occasion where the transfer\ncountry proposes varied terms. This means the Attorney-General can\nconsider whether to refuse consent under subsection (1) in relation to\neach variation of terms proposed by the transfer country.\n\nSchedule 6  Refusal of consent to international transfer of prisoners\n\nCrimes and Other Legislation Amendment (Omnibus) Act 2023\nNo. 63, 2023\n(4) If the Attorney-General decides under subsection (1) to refuse\nconsent to the transfer on terms proposed by the transfer country,\nsections 20 to 23 do not apply for the transfer on those particular\nterms.\nNote:\nSections 20 to 23 continue to apply for the transfer on any proposed\nvariation of those terms, unless the Attorney-General has refused\nconsent to the varied terms under subsection (1) of this section."
    },
